<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/expert/nanomaterials-and-biomaterials">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nanomaterials and biomaterials are the ladder\'s frontier pair: <strong>smallness as a
    property-creating dimension</strong>, and <strong>the living body as a design
    environment</strong>. Both fields invert the classical order — instead of bulk properties
    inherited from composition, properties are engineered from architecture at scales biology
    always worked at.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="scale effects, nanocarbons, nature\'s patents, the body\'s medium" widget="equationStep" config={nanoCfg} />

  <h2>Delivered and coming</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Already everywhere:</strong> lithium-battery electrodes (nano-structured for surface area — the energy lesson\'s storage), drug nanoparticles (better solubility, targeting), catalysts (nano- particulates run every chemical plant), sunscreen (nano-zinc oxide — transparent yet UV-blocking).</li>
    <li><strong>Safety is part of the engineering:</strong> small particles move and react unusually — nanotoxicology studies exposure and clearance (the testing gaps echo the chemical-society debates: measure before deploying). Regulation now treats size as a material property, which it is.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical devices at the frontier:</strong> drug-eluting stents, artificial joints lasting decades (the friction lesson\'s bio-tribology), neural interfaces (the neuroscience lesson\'s BCIs — materials compatibility decides whether the probe scars or reads), and dissolving electronics for temporary sensors.</li>
    <li><strong>The synthesis problem:</strong> making nano-structures at scale, uniformly (the chip lesson\'s precision, generalised): self-assembly (chemistry doing the patterning) is the field\'s big bet — the next lesson gives it the computational engine.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Geim & Novoselov on graphene; the biomimetics literature (Barth, Fratzl); Ratner et al., Biomaterials Science.</p>
</TopicPage>
